{"feature_id": "duckdb.datatype.time", "feature_type": "datatype", "dbms": "duckdb", "doc_version": "stable", "name": "TIME", "normalized_name": "time", "type_name": "TIME", "category": "", "signature": "time", "syntax": [{"pattern": "time", "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}], "aliases": [], "description_claims": [{"text": "time of day (no time zone)", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "The TIME and TIMETZ types specify the hour, minute, second, microsecond of a day.", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "Conversion between Strings and Naïve / Time Zone-Aware Timestamps", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "The conversion between strings without UTC offsets or IANA time zone names and WITHOUT TIME ZONE types is unambiguous and straightforward. The conversion between strings with UTC offsets or time zone", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "1970-01-01 00:00:00[+00] (Unix system time zero)", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "Time Zones", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "To understand time zones and the WITH TIME ZONE types, it helps to start with two concepts: instants and temporal binning.", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "An instant is a point in absolute time, usually given as a count of some time increment from a fixed point in time (called the epoch). This is similar to how positions on the earth's surface are given", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "Temporal binning rules are complex, and generally come in two sets: time zones and calendars. For most tasks, the calendar will just be the widely used Gregorian calendar, but time zones apply locale-", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "Time Zone Support", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}, {"text": "To set the time zone to use, first load the ICU extension. The ICU extension comes pre-bundled with several DuckDB clients (including Python, R, JDBC and ODBC), so this step can be skipped in those ca", "source": "rule", "confidence": 0.85, "evidence_refs": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002_b0017"]}], "examples": [], "constraints": [], "attributes": {}, "source_sections": ["duckdb_stable_overview_s0002", "duckdb_stable_time_s0001", "duckdb_stable_time_s0002", "duckdb_stable_timestamp_s0003", "duckdb_stable_timestamp_s0004"], "source_anchors": [], "candidate_group_id": "duckdb.datatype.time", "status": "validated"}
